Business owners are often encouraged to define their “why.” In fact, ever since Simon Sinek's famous TED Talk, business pros have been asking themselves:
Why do I do what I do? Why does my business exist? Why should customers care?
Those are useful questions, but they can also lead to broad answers that sound suspiciously like everyone else’s. You want to serve people. You care about quality. You hope to make a difference.
All true, perhaps, but not especially revealing or compelling when everyone else is saying almost the same thing--minus an audience difference. "We help working moms...", "We help teachers...," etc.
There may be another way to get to something much deeper and personal to help you become more intriguing to your ideal audience.
‍  ‍
How did I end up in this business?
Not the polished version. Not the story rewritten years later to make every step look intentional. What actually brought you here?
For example, Mark Manson, author of The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F*ck, wrote about his origin story in a recent newsletter. He explained he wanted to be a musician. He loved the adoration of being on stage. But the practicing and craft part was boring so he found himself on internet groups arguing about things like which band had the best drummer.
At first he thought he was procrastinating, but then he realized that was what he really loved--writing, thinking, and debating.
Maybe you loved the work before you ever considered turning it into a business. Maybe someone saw your talent and encouraged you to charge for it. Perhaps you noticed a problem no one else was solving or you had a friend or family member that needed something and you provided a solution then realized they were not the only one with this challenge. You may have inherited a company, bought into an opportunity, left a job that no longer fit, or built something because you needed more control over your time and future.
However it happened, the path into your business often reveals what you value most now.
‍  ‍
Your Beginning Leaves Fingerprints
Businesses tend to carry the fingerprints of their beginnings.
Someone who started a company after receiving terrible service may become almost obsessive about communication and responsiveness.
A person who built a business from a hobby may care deeply about craftsmanship, creativity, and the details customers do not always notice.
An entrepreneur who entered an industry because a mentor opened a door may place unusual importance on relationships, guidance, and creating opportunities for others.
Someone who started a business out of financial necessity may build a company that is resourceful, practical, and highly attentive to value.
These aren't just interesting details from the past. They often flavor the customer experience, company culture, services, hiring decisions, and standards the business owner refuses to compromise.
Your origin story may explain why you answer emails quickly, spend extra time educating customers, customize every project, hire people without traditional experience, or insist on making your service easier to understand.
The beginning is often where the business learned what mattered.
‍  ‍
Every Origin Story Has a Strength and a Risk
Looking back can also help you recognize where your beginning may still be shaping the business in less helpful ways.
If you started because you loved doing the work, you may be known for quality and expertise. But you may also struggle to delegate because no one does it quite the way you do.
If your first customers came through word of mouth, your business may be built on strong relationships. But you may have avoided creating a consistent marketing strategy because referrals worked for so long.
‍If you started by saying yes to every opportunity, your flexibility may still be one of your strengths. It may also have left you with too many services, unclear positioning, or customers who don't fully understand what you do best.
Your beginning can be a compass, but it shouldn't become a cage.
Don't abandon the qualities that helped you build the business. Understand which ones still serve you and which ones need to evolve.
‍  ‍
Ask Better Questions to Mold Your Business Story
Start with these questions:
‍  ‍
What first drew or pushed you into this business?
Was it curiosity, talent, frustration, necessity, opportunity, or encouragement from someone else?
‍  ‍
What belief did that experience create?
Perhaps you decided customers deserved clearer answers, better options, more personal service, or a different kind of experience.
‍  ‍
Where can customers see that belief today?
Look at your policies, services, communication style, pricing, team culture, and customer relationships.

Then ask one more question:
  ‍
Is the way you started still guiding the business, or is it limiting what comes next?
That answer can help you make better decisions about growth without losing what made the business meaningful in the first place.
‍  ‍
Turn Your Beginning Into a Better Business Story
Once you understand the connection between how you began and how you operate today, you have the foundation for a much stronger About page.
‍  ‍
Too many About pages are either thin résumés or generic company descriptions. They list years of experience, certifications, services, and a few well-behaved adjectives. They tell visitors what the company is, but not why it became this particular kind of business.
A stronger personalized business story connects three things:
‍  ‍
The beginning: What brought you into the      work?
The belief: What did that experience      teach you?
The customer impact: How does that belief      shape the way you serve people today?
‍  ‍
For example, instead of saying, “We're committed to excellent customer service,” explain that the business was created after you experienced how confusing and impersonal the industry could be. That frustration led you to build a company known for clear communication, education, and fewer unpleasant surprises.
Now the reader understands where the promise came from and it sets you apart from everyone else that claims "the best service out there."
Don't try to craft a dramatic founder story. Focus on sharing an honest one.
The opportunity, challenge, interest, or open door that brought you into business says more about your company than your marketing team's carefully crafted mission statement.
When you understand that story, you can use it to guide your decisions, strengthen your marketing, and create an About page that helps customers see not only what you do, but why you do it the way you do. And that can be a powerful connector.

‍  ‍
Christina Metcalf is a writer and women’s speaker who believes in the power of story. She works with small businesses, chambers of commerce, and business professionals who want to make an impression and grow a loyal customer/member base. She is the author of The Glinda Principle, rediscovering the magic within.
